17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,69,3,387,264,729,4633,279,2477,42224,25780,311,279,6785,25780,1741,429,400,69,7,15,8,284,282,7,16,8,284,220,16,3,323,198,59,26056,37018,90,69,1445,8,282,1255,488,220,16,9139,90,69,1255,9139,488,1124,37018,90,69,1445,8,282,1445,481,296,9139,90,69,1445,481,296,481,220,16,9139,284,282,1445,488,220,16,10699,60,1958,678,2477,42224,25780,400,77,3,323,400,76,4779,1380,400,77,1124,709,296,488,220,16,2418,271,9885,279,24632,2477,42224,7546,400,77,3,1741,429,400,69,1445,8,861,220,16,15,61,21,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $f$ be a function taking the nonnegative integers to the positive integers such that $f(0) = f(1) = 1$ and\n\\[\\frac{f(n) f(m + 1)}{f(m)} + \\frac{f(n) f(n - m)}{f(n - m - 1)} = f(n + 1)\\]for all nonnegative integers $n$ and $m,$ where $n \\ge m + 1.$\n\nFind the smallest nonnegative integer $n$ such that $f(n) > 10^6.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9594eba804cf4c69979eb60f180c300e21b879c8c9f23964d49918455a0825ad"},"seconds":63.03925037384033,"seed":131616101777424833,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given functional equation and the initial conditions. The given equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(n) f(m + 1)}{f(m)} + \\frac{f(n) f(n - m)}{f(n - m - 1)} = f(n + 1)\n\\]\n\nfor all nonnegative integers \\(n\\) and \\(m\\), where \\(n \\ge m + 1\\). The initial conditions are \\(f(0) = f(1) = 1\\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the case when \\(m = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(n) f(1)}{f(0)} + \\frac{f(n) f(n)}{f(n - 1)} = f(n + 1)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(0) = 1\\) and \\(f(1) = 1\\), this simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nf(n) + \\frac{f(n)^2}{f(n - 1)} = f(n + 1)\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\nf(n + 1) = f(n) + \\frac{f(n)^2}{f(n - 1)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we will use the initial values to find the values of \\(f(2)\\), \\(f(3)\\), etc. Starting with \\(n = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nf(2) = f(1) + \\frac{f(1)^2}{f(0)} = 1 + \\frac{1^2}{1} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, for \\(n = 2\\):\n\n\\[\nf(3) = f(2) + \\frac{f(2)^2}{f(1)} = 2 + \\frac{2^2}{1} = 2 + 4 = 6\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 3\\):\n\n\\[\nf(4) = f(3) + \\frac{f(3)^2}{f(2)} = 6 + \\frac{6^2}{2} = 6 + 18 = 24\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 4\\):\n\n\\[\nf(5) = f(4) + \\frac{f(4)^2}{f(3)} = 24 + \\frac{24^2}{6} = 24 + 96 = 120\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 5\\):\n\n\\[\nf(6) = f(5) + \\frac{f(5)^2}{f(4)} = 120 + \\frac{120^2}{24} = 120 + 600 = 720\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 6\\):\n\n\\[\nf(7) = f(6) + \\frac{f(6)^2}{f(5)} = 720 + \\frac{720^2}{120} = 720 + 4320 = 5040\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 7\\):\n\n\\[\nf(8) = f(7) + \\frac{f(7)^2}{f(6)} = 5040 + \\frac{5040^2}{720} = 5040 + 35280 = 40320\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 8\\):\n\n\\[\nf(9) = f(8) + \\frac{f(8)^2}{f(7)} = 40320 + \\frac{40320^2}{5040} = 40320 + 322560 = 362880\n\\]\n\nWe see that \\(f(n) = n!\\) satisfies the given functional equation. We need to find the smallest \\(n\\) such that \\(n! > 10^6\\). We have:\n\n\\[\n7! = 5040 < 10^6\n\\]\n\\[\n8! = 40320 < 10^6\n\\]\n\\[\n9! = 362880 < 10^6\n\\]\n\\[\n10! = 3628800 > 10^6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest nonnegative integer \\(n\\) such that \\(f(n) > 10^6\\) is \\(n = 10\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{10}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
